130
00:15:44,960 --> 00:15:52,080
Inhabited continuously for over 10,000 years, Jericho was the scene of

131
00:15:52,080 --> 00:15:58,440
crucial innovations, such as the practice of agriculture and the construction of the first

132
00:15:58,440 --> 00:16:05,320
stone fortifications. Its soil holds layer upon layer of civilizations, each built on

133
00:16:05,320 --> 00:16:14,560
the remains of the one before it, weaving an unbroken history that resonates to this day.

134
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:19,440
... The walls of Jericho,

135
00:16:19,440 --> 00:16:26,720
erected some 8,000 years ago, are among the oldest defensive structures uncovered

136
00:16:26,720 --> 00:16:29,520
by archaeology. ...

137
00:16:32,680 --> 00:16:38,680
According to biblical tradition, its walls crumbled at the sound of trumpets led

138
00:16:38,680 --> 00:16:45,200
by Joshua, enabling the Israelite conquest of Canaan. In the Gospels,

139
00:16:45,200 --> 00:16:49,880
Jericho appears as the scene of two significant encounters:

140
00:16:49,880 --> 00:16:56,840
Jesus restored sight to a blind man at the city gates and transformed the life of Zacchaeus,

141
00:16:56,840 --> 00:17:01,840
a tax collector who climbed a tree to watch him pass by.

142
00:17:01,840 --> 00:17:09,320
But how has this city survived for millennia in the middle of such an arid desert?

143
00:17:09,320 --> 00:17:14,680
The answer lies in its natural secret:

144
00:17:14,680 --> 00:17:22,320
"water." Jericho is an oasis where life flourishes even amid extreme aridity.

145
00:17:22,320 --> 00:17:29,160
Natural springs, such as the Elisha Spring, combined with ancient irrigation practices,

146
00:17:29,160 --> 00:17:36,120
have sustained the city for thousands of years. A continuing miracle of resilience in the heart

147
00:17:36,120 --> 00:17:42,960
of the dry land.

148
00:17:42,960 --> 00:17:49,680
But Jericho is not just a miracle of human history. Around this unlikely oasis,

149
00:17:49,680 --> 00:17:55,760
in the vast silence of the Judean Desert, wildlife has also found its own

150
00:17:55,760 --> 00:18:01,360
ways to endure and flourish. ...

151
00:18:03,120 --> 00:18:10,440
Imagine the golden cliffs under the scorching sun: there, on the edges of the rocks,

152
00:18:10,440 --> 00:18:17,440
the desert ibex defies gravity. With their hooves adapted to grip the smallest

153
00:18:17,440 --> 00:18:25,560
rock reliefs, these magnificent wild goats scale steep walls as

154
00:18:25,560 --> 00:18:33,240
if walking on solid ground. Few know it, but their leaps can reach more than two meters

155
00:18:33,240 --> 00:18:40,120
in height, a vital skill for escaping predators and surviving in such

156
00:18:40,120 --> 00:18:47,120
inhospitable terrain. ...

157
00:18:47,120 --> 00:18:54,160
When the heat of the day dissipates and night covers the desert with its cold blanket, the barn owl

158
00:18:54,160 --> 00:19:02,560
awakens. Its light plumage blends with the pallor of the sand under the starlight.

159
00:19:02,560 --> 00:19:09,480
And there's a fascinating detail: its wings are structured in such a way as to almost

160
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:14,720
completely reduce the sound of flight, allowing it to approach its prey undetected

161
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:21,400
. With its watchful eyes and keen hearing, it detects the slightest movement on

162
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:28,920
the ground. It is a patient hunter! "And in the desert, patience is the key to

163
00:19:28,920 --> 00:19:31,760
survival." ...

164
00:19:34,160 --> 00:19:41,760
When the first ray of sunlight touches the desert rocks, the "Egyptian vulture" appears in the sky:

165
00:19:41,760 --> 00:19:50,520
small, agile, and much more intelligent than its appearance suggests. Unlike other vultures,

166
00:19:50,520 --> 00:19:56,000
It is one of the few animals on the planet known to use tools.

167
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:02,440
With carefully selected stones, it cracks ostrich eggs for food,

168
00:20:02,440 --> 00:20:10,040
a rare scene that few human eyes have witnessed. In a world where only

169
00:20:10,040 --> 00:20:18,540
the most cunning and resilient prosper, the desert becomes the greatest of tests.

170
00:20:18,540 --> 00:20:23,360
... There are landscapes that seem to defy the

171
00:20:23,360 --> 00:20:33,040
logic of human existence, and the Negev Desert, on the shores of the Dead Sea, is one of them.

172
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:37,920
... Despite its arid

173
00:20:37,920 --> 00:20:45,320
and inhospitable appearance, this territory has been a refuge for resilient civilizations that flourished where

174
00:20:45,320 --> 00:20:52,520
few would dare to remain. From nomadic tribes and Nabataean traders, to

175
00:20:52,520 --> 00:20:59,920
ancient Egyptian miners, Christian pilgrims, and modern adventurers, surviving here has always

176
00:20:59,920 --> 00:21:07,440
been a matter of adaptation and creativity. ...

177
00:21:07,440 --> 00:21:14,680
Amidst the brutal heat of the Negev, an unexpected animal appears. It attacks the very logic of the

178
00:21:14,680 --> 00:21:21,800
desert with speed and fury: "the caracal." ...

179
00:21:21,800 --> 00:21:28,440
At the slightest sign of movement, it springs like a compressed spring: it can leap more than three

180
00:21:28,440 --> 00:21:35,760
meters, catching birds in mid-flight as if defying gravity. Its hind muscles,

181
00:21:35,760 --> 00:21:42,920
tense like bows ready to fire, are weapons of survival in a terrain that punishes

182
00:21:42,920 --> 00:21:46,440
any hesitation. ...

183
00:21:53,800 --> 00:22:01,440
And when we look up, the desert also reveals predators coming from the sky. The

184
00:22:01,440 --> 00:22:08,240
peregrine falcon, owner of the fastest dive in the animal kingdom, swoops down from the

185
00:22:08,240 --> 00:22:17,960
sky at more than 300 km/h, a winged lightning bolt, silent and lethal. Its eyes detect movements at great

186
00:22:17,960 --> 00:22:26,440
distances, and its wings close like blades. In the air, it reigns supreme: gravity itself

187
00:22:26,440 --> 00:22:33,120
bends before its precision. ...

188
00:22:33,120 --> 00:22:40,560
But not all survival requires speed or strength. Some creatures have overcome the desert

189
00:22:40,560 --> 00:22:48,040
with strategy and metamorphosis. This is the case of the common chameleon, a master of adaptation

190
00:22:48,040 --> 00:22:54,800
that defies the logic of camouflage. ...

191
00:22:54,800 --> 00:23:01,160
Contrary to what many think, it doesn't change color just to hide.

192
00:23:01,160 --> 00:23:08,720
It “speaks” through its skin: light shades to ward off the heat, dark shades to absorb

193
00:23:08,720 --> 00:23:12,920
the sun’s energy in the mornings. ...

194
00:23:14,840 --> 00:23:18,880
And its eyes? They’re veritable watchtowers:

195
00:23:18,880 --> 00:23:25,240
each can focus in opposite directions at the same time, locating prey while keeping an eye out

196
00:23:25,240 --> 00:23:30,480
for predators. ...

197
00:23:30,480 --> 00:23:33,320
With a tongue faster than the blink of an eye,

198
00:23:33,320 --> 00:23:39,440
the chameleon captures insects at surprising distances, a lethal extension of its

199
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:44,160
own body. ...

200
00:23:44,160 --> 00:23:51,320
Every desert creature is living proof that, even in the most inhospitable setting,

201
00:23:51,320 --> 00:23:59,280
nature not only survives, but defies limits. Now, can you imagine floating effortlessly

202
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:06,520
across the waters of a lake where nothing survives? A place where the water burns your eyes

203
00:24:06,520 --> 00:24:10,920
but heals your skin? ...

204
00:24:12,280 --> 00:24:19,480
It may sound strange, but this is where it happens: 430 meters below sea level

205
00:24:19,480 --> 00:24:28,520
lies the lowest point on land: the Dead Sea.

206
00:24:28,520 --> 00:24:33,080
...Although it is called a "sea,"

207
00:24:33,080 --> 00:24:40,680
it is actually an extremely salty lake, with a salt concentration ten times

208
00:24:40,680 --> 00:24:47,880
greater than that of the oceans. This characteristic makes it totally hostile to life,

209
00:24:47,880 --> 00:24:54,120
but fascinating for the history of humanity, since, for millennia, it has become

210
00:24:54,120 --> 00:25:02,160
a place of mystery, healing, and veneration. ...

211
00:25:04,320 --> 00:25:11,200
The ancient Egyptians used the minerals of the Dead Sea to embalm the dead,

212
00:25:11,200 --> 00:25:18,320
while stories tell that Cleopatra exploited its resources to preserve its beauty,

213
00:25:18,320 --> 00:25:24,960
resorting to the mud and mineral salts unique to the region. King Herod,

214
00:25:24,960 --> 00:25:31,760
for his part, recognizing the unique value of this landscape, ordered the construction of luxurious palaces on its

215
00:25:31,760 --> 00:25:40,080
shores. Today, travelers from all over the world head to the Dead Sea in search of its benefits:

216
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:48,280
they float in its dense waters and rest on the black, mineral-rich mud, all amidst

217
00:25:48,280 --> 00:25:55,240
a surreal, dreamlike setting. ...

218
00:25:55,240 --> 00:26:01,200
For thousands of years, the Jordan River fed the Dead Sea with its waters.

219
00:26:01,200 --> 00:26:09,280
But starting in the 1960s, Israel, Jordan and Syria began to divert most of

220
00:26:09,280 --> 00:26:17,520
Part of that flow goes to supply cities and plantations. Today, less than 5% of the water

221
00:26:17,520 --> 00:26:25,200
that once reached the Dead Sea still flows into it. Without sufficient replenishment and with

222
00:26:25,200 --> 00:26:32,840
intense evaporation, the lake level recedes more than a meter each year. As the

223
00:26:32,840 --> 00:26:40,080
Dead Sea level continues to drop, huge holes appear in the ground, called "sinkholes."

224
00:26:40,640 --> 00:26:47,680
These collapse the ground, forming craters that swallow roads, plantations, and even

225
00:26:47,680 --> 00:26:53,640
buildings. If this process continues unchecked, in a few decades the Dead Sea

226
00:26:53,640 --> 00:27:00,880
could shrink so much that only small, isolated lagoons would remain, and the landscape we know today

227
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:09,120
could become a mere memory. ...

228
00:27:09,120 --> 00:27:15,960
For now, the Dead Sea endures, a silent witness to the passing of time. Its motionless waters

229
00:27:15,960 --> 00:27:22,760
hold fragments of a time when gigantic rivers fed it and

230
00:27:22,760 --> 00:27:31,160
cities flourished on its shores. Today it reflects the sky like a forgotten mirror, a silent reminder that

231
00:27:31,160 --> 00:27:37,280
there the rules that govern the world surrender to ancient, unseen forces,

232
00:27:37,280 --> 00:27:45,440
shaped by fire, earth, and the memory of the past. Now imagine yourself trapped on a

233
00:27:45,440 --> 00:27:53,200
rocky mountaintop, staring into the horizon, where thousands of enemy soldiers are advancing mercilessly.

234
00:27:53,720 --> 00:28:03,780
There is no way out. No reinforcements. Only freedom remains... or submission.

235
00:28:03,780 --> 00:28:07,640
... That was the

236
00:28:07,640 --> 00:28:15,800
choice facing nearly a thousand Jewish men, women, and children almost two thousand years ago.

237
00:28:16,320 --> 00:28:23,520
It is here, in the scorching heart of the Judean Desert, on the shores of the Dead Sea, that the story

238
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:33,480
of Masada stands as a cry of resistance. ...

239
00:28:33,480 --> 00:28:40,120
Built in the first century BC by King Herod, Masada was an isolated fortress,

240
00:28:40,120 --> 00:28:48,320
almost impregnable, with palaces, storehouses, and cisterns hewn from the rock to survive

241
00:28:48,320 --> 00:28:54,600
any siege. ...

242
00:28:54,600 --> 00:29:01,800
But it was during the First Jewish Revolt that it became the scene of one of the

243
00:29:01,800 --> 00:29:10,960
most dramatic episodes in history: there, some 960 Jews, cornered by the troops of the ruthless

244
00:29:10,960 --> 00:29:20,760
Roman Empire, made an extreme decision: they preferred collective death to slavery.

245
00:29:20,760 --> 00:29:26,520
...An act that turned Masada into

246
00:29:26,520 --> 00:29:33,440
a powerful symbol of resistance and freedom. Today, walking among the ruins of this

247
00:29:33,440 --> 00:29:40,640
fortress, especially at dawn, is like walking through the living pages of one of the

248
00:29:40,640 --> 00:29:44,920
most shocking episodes in ancient history. ...

249
00:29:50,040 --> 00:29:54,840
Continuing south, the desert begins to curve. To undulate before

250
00:29:54,840 --> 00:30:01,000
our eyes. And then an almost surreal vision opens before us:

251
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:05,360
"Makhtesh Ramon." ...

252
00:30:06,520 --> 00:30:14,600
A colossal crater. Forty kilometers long. Ten kilometers wide. Sculpted

253
00:30:14,600 --> 00:30:22,600
not by the fury of a volcano. Nor by the brutal impact of a meteorite. Makhtesh is something even

254
00:30:22,600 --> 00:30:30,320
rarer. A masterpiece of erosion. A geological phenomenon almost exclusive to this forgotten corner

255
00:30:30,320 --> 00:30:34,480
of the world. ...

256
00:30:34,480 --> 00:30:41,000
And so we come to the end of our incredible journey through Israel and Palestine. If you enjoyed the video

257
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:47,440
and loved the wonders we explored together, please give it a thumbs up and help

258
00:30:47,440 --> 00:30:51,440
our channel grow. Thanks and have a safe trip!